######
#
# Libatsynmark: marking the syntax of ATS2
#
######

libatsynmark is a library of functions for marking the (level-1) syntax of
ATS2 source: the tokens (lexbufobj_get_tokenlst), the position-synmarks of
the tokens and of the syntax trees (listize_token2psynmark,
lexbufobj_level1_psynmarkize), the XHTML of a piece of code
(string_pats2xhtmlize_bground, string_pats2xhtmlize_embedded and the charlst
versions), and the declarations of a file for building the ATSLIB documentation
(charlst_declitemize, d0eclreplst_find_synop). The interface is
SATS/libatsynmark.sats. pats2xhtml (../atsynmark) is built on it.

It is written in ATS2 without GC and is built by patsopt and the C compiler on
top of the modules of the compiler (src/pats_lexing, pats_tokbuf, pats_parsing,
pats_syntax, ...) and of libatsopt.a, the compiler as a library: ATS1 is not
needed. The differences from the ATS1 version are listed at the top of
SATS/libatsynmark.sats and in src/PORTING.md ("pats2xhtml and libatsynmark").

######

Building:

  make                  # BUILD/libatsynmark.a (see Makefile_build, Makefile_vars)
  make install          # copies it to $(PATSHOME)/ccomp/atslib/lib
  make check            # typechecks the files
  make test             # TEST/: test01 test02 test03 and the md5 suite (python3)

A program that uses it links with libatsynmark.a, libatsopt.a and -lgmp (-lgmp
is not needed if libatsopt.a was built with C3NSTRINTKND=intknd):

  patscc -DATS_MEMALLOC_LIBC -D_GNU_SOURCE -include alloca.h -I$PATSHOME/src \
    -o prog prog.dats -L$PATSHOME/utils/libatsynmark/BUILD -latsynmark \
    -L$PATSHOME/src -latsopt -lgmp

and calls libatsynmark_dynloadall() before anything else (it initializes
libatsopt; the ATS1 version had `dynload "utils/libatsynmark/dynloadall.dats"`).

TEST/: test01 prints the tokens of STDIN, test02 the position-synmarks, test03
calls the functions of the API on a file (see its header). The md5 suite
(TEST/suite.py, expected.md5, cases/) runs pats2xhtml, test01, test02 and test03 on
the edge cases that it generates (unterminated comments, strings and chars, every kind
of number, directives, external code, unicode, CRLF, very long lines, the command line of
pats2xhtml, ...) and on a few real files, in a temporary directory with the locale C, and
compares the md5 of the results with expected.md5, which was produced by the ATS1 build of
the same programs: the suite does not depend on the directory of the checkout, the locale,
or ATS1. `suite.py update` regenerates expected.md5 from ATS1 builds.

###### end of [README for Libatsynmark] ######
